 # Cache-based Lazy Memory Management
 
-Also called: CLM (Cache-based Lazy Memory management)
+Also called: CLM
 
 ## Overview
-Cache-based Lazy Memory Management (CLM) is a memory offloading strategy that enables the training of large-scale 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes on a single consumer-grade GPU by keeping the majority of Gaussian primitives in CPU memory and loading only the subset accessed by the current training view [S1]. It distinguishes itself from naive offloading approaches (like Zero-Offload) by exploiting the inherent sparsity of 3DGS memory access patterns, using ahead-of-time view-Gaussian mapping, caching overlapping primitives between views, and scheduling iterations to maximize spatial locality, thereby overlapping communication with computation to hide CPU-GPU transfer overheads [S1].
+Cache-based Lazy Memory Management (CLM) is a memory offloading strategy that enables the training of large-scale 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes on a single consumer-grade GPU by exploiting the inherent sparsity of Gaussian memory access patterns [S1]. Unlike naive offloading approaches that load all Gaussians to the GPU or multi-GPU solutions that require complex synchronization, CLM keeps the majority of Gaussians in CPU memory and loads only the subset accessed by the current view, overlapping communication with computation to hide latency [S1].
 
 ## How it works
+CLM operates on the standard 3D Gaussian primitive definition provided by [Rendering Implementation and Acceleration](21069-rendering-implementation-and-acceleration.md). The method manages the lifecycle of Gaussian parameters between CPU RAM and GPU VRAM to fit scenes exceeding GPU capacity.
+
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-**Inputs:**
-- A set of posed training images (views) with known camera parameters.
-- A large collection of 3D Gaussians representing the scene, stored primarily in CPU memory.
-- The current GPU memory capacity (e.g., 24 GB for RTX 4090).
+- **Input:** A set of posed training images (views), the full list of $N$ 3D Gaussians stored in CPU memory, and the current optimizer state.
+- **Output:** Updated Gaussian parameters in CPU memory (and optionally GPU memory for the active subset) after a training iteration.
 
-**Outputs:**
-- Updated Gaussian parameters (position, covariance, spherical harmonics, opacity) optimized via gradient descent.
-- Rendered images for the current training view.
